WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:13.369
Calcium is the third element in group two
on the periodic table. It’s really very

00:00:13.369 --> 00:00:23.230
common. We find it all over the world in all
sorts of minerals. So for example you can

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:29.230
have bones, this is a pelvis of a sheep, I
found it when I was walking one day, you can

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:31.840
see the legs go in there and there.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:39.440
In here we have some calcium turnings. We’re
storing them in our glove box. This glove

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.969
box here is a contraption full of nitrogen,
it’s dry nitrogen so that we can keep our

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:50.530
calcium away from oxygen and away from moisture
as well, because obviously what we don’t

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:56.359
want is when we get our nice very reactive
calcium surface we don’t want it to tarnish

00:00:56.359 --> 00:01:04.500
from oxides too quickly before we can react
it with things. So what we have here inside

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:12.170
the bottle are turnings of calcium. Calcium
is quite hard compared to sort of the heavier

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.540
group two elements, so strontium and barium,
so it’s harder to file.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:24.790
Bones are made from calcium phosphate mixed
with some organic material as well and they’re

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:32.120
very strong because of the structure within
the bones. There are all sorts of shells of

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:39.720
molluscs and the like where they use calcium
carbonate from the sea water, getting the

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:48.259
calcium out, reacting with carbon dioxide
and making their shells. And because shells

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:55.259
are made out of calcium carbonate, which dissolves
in acid, people are quite worried about the

00:01:55.259 --> 00:02:05.039
effects of global warming. Not the heating
up but when the C02 dissolves into the sea

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:13.049
it makes it more acid, so in principle shells
could start dissolving and some molluscs may

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:16.200
not be able to survive because they just can’t
make their shells.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.790
If I just pour them out so you can see.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:28.010
Calcium looks metallic than the calcium metal
itself because of the way the electrons behave

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:36.160
in the solid. In fact it is because it is
a very good electrical conductor but the compounds

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:45.350
are all white, because calcium has no free
electrons that can go from one energy level

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:52.660
to another in the way you can say with copper.
So copper sulphate is a beautiful blue colour,

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:58.900
calcium sulphate is white, and it’s actually
colourless, so if you see a big crystal of

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:04.979
calcium sulphate it just looks like glass.
But the white is caused by very small crystals

00:03:04.979 --> 00:03:11.030
which scatter the light, just in the same
way that any sort of white powder will scatter

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:16.419
light, flour for example or talcum powder,
anything like that.

00:03:16.419 --> 00:03:23.060
Calcium compounds themselves are actually
mostly white, but calcium metal itself, as

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:29.049
you can see we’ve got tarnished parts but
also there are some shiny bits where it’s

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:33.650
untarnished. So you’re actually seeing the
metal the lustre of the metal there as well.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:42.390
Calcium carbonate dissolves in water saturated
with C02 to make calcium bicarbonate which

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:51.639
is more soluble. And so you can get water,
so called hard water, that has quite high

00:03:51.639 --> 00:03:59.440
levels of calcium. And when this percolates
through the ground and comes to a cave it

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:07.420
can then crystallise out and you can get hanging
down pillars of calcium carbonate, which are

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:14.160
called stalactites. And you can have pillars
growing from the floor called stalagmites.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:21.100
This is a stalagmite from Turkey. One of my
friends called Hugh was really naughty. He

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:29.700
knew I was interested in minerals and he broke
this off from a cave in Turkey about 45 years

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:35.250
ago and brought it and gave it to me so I
was really pleased. And you can see the outside

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:41.770
looks quite blobby, and inside you can see
there are nice crystals of calcium carbonate

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:49.760
because it has grown very, very slowly and
so it can crystalise. And sometimes these

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:56.400
stalagmites and stalactites can grow really
big. And more recently, in the last few years,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.430
people have found huge crystals of calcium
sulphate under the ground. Some of you may

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:07.310
have seen pictures of caves in Mexico where
there are crystals big enough you can actually

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:14.260
walk along them. So there is no danger that
calcium will ever run out, it’s the basis

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:21.100
of cements and concretes, or one of the bases
because then you need other elements like

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:29.160
iron and silica to make the concrete itself,
but calcium is one of the principle components.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:33.740
We’re going to take calcium chloride, put
it in a flame and do a flame test and see

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:38.940
if we can see the colour of the calcium in
the flame. So as I said before about calcium

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:44.160
compounds, calcium compounds are often white,
and here is a white calcium compound. So I’m

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:51.990
going to put it in the flame and see if we
get any colour. Oh hello, that looks quite

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:59.090
red doesn’t it? Brick red is the classic
calcium colour. Bit orangey to me, might be

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:07.530
a bit of sodium in there, or something like
that. That was our calcium flame test.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:13.240
The reason that you don’t find lumps of
calcium metal lying around, the way you do

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:25.510
with copper or gold, is that metallic calcium
is very reactive, reacts with water very quickly.

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:32.120
Magnesium doesn’t react because it has a
layer of oxide on the surface, so surprisingly

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:38.690
magnesium is not nearly as reactive as calcium.
You can use magnesium, or its alloys, for

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:44.270
the wheels on your car, go round really fast
because they’re light. You couldn’t have

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:49.860
calcium wheels in your car, well you could,
but the first time it rained your whole car

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.750
would go up in flames.

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:55.630
This again what we’ve got is our calcium
chloride, hydrated calcium chloride. We’ve

00:06:55.630 --> 00:07:03.240
got a bunch of it on this spatula. I’ll
put it in the flame so that you can see that

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.690
it’s burning with a brick red flame which
is characteristic of calcium. It’s kind

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:14.540
of a bit orangey to me as well actually, might
be a bit of sodium or something in there.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:21.419
Got anymore in there? A little bit.

00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:27.240
Many people see calcium as a very English
element, unlike strontium which was discovered

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:33.710
in Scotland, because of the white cliffs of
Dover, the gateway for many people when they

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:41.890
first come to England. They’re made of calcium
carbonate. And so this white colour of chalk

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:48.250
is very characteristic and all over the southern
part of England you find large amounts of

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:57.050
chalk, which in the north of England they
tend to be limestone, which is also calcium

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:01.680
carbonate but a slightly different variety
of calcium carbonate so it’s a yellow or

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.860
honey colour because of the impurities of
iron or some other element that’s in it.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:13.590
Well you need calcium for all sorts of processes
in the body and of course to build up your

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:22.890
bones. Particularly, say in the case of pregnant
women, they need to have calcium for the bones

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:30.510
of the developing baby. But as people get
older the calcium can come out of their bones

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:39.570
and make them very fragile, this is why elderly
people sometimes break their hips and break

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:47.050
their bones so easily. So drinking milk and
eating other sources of calcium are good because

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:51.690
it helps you replenish your bones and keep
a reasonable level of calcium.

